      <frontmatter>
        <section role="acknowledgments"> 
          Adrian, first: I love you. Every day I try to think of ways to thank you for encouraging me to keep going through the toughest of times, and for the invaluable feedback you provided even though this book has been… not quite your cup of tea. Thank you. You are the best thing to ever happen to me.

          I wish also to thank Rambling Chantrix, who proved to me that I have an audience that will listen to everything I have to say then ask for more. She is the author of many vibrant and surreal-yet-heartfelt (and sexy) stories, including her debut short story omnibus, <work medium="book">Sad Porn About the End of the World</work>; more can be read about her work on her professional website (<work medium="site">rambling.chantrix.org</work>).

          Finally, a big <quote>thank you so much</quote> goes to Dirk Hooper, who cheated death just so that he could give this book the cover it deserves; his creative services can be accessed from his professional website (<work medium="site">www.dirkhooper.com</work>).
        </section>

        <section role="dedication">
          This story is dedicated to them, Murphy, Skye, Rachel, Effie… and Lexi, wherever she may be.
        </section>
        
        <section role="authors-note" about="semantic text formatting">
          <heading>Semantic Formatting Key</heading>
          <subheading>
            A Note to the Reader on the Formatting of this text
          </subheading>

          The voiced or unvoiced qualities of thoughts, memories, fantasies, whispers, and nonverbal gestures ought to be either implied or denoted explicitly by the bare text, but they have been further hinted at by choice of punctuation, font face, and color:

          <narrative>
            My case queue was empty. There was nothing for me to do. I leaned back, closed my eyes, and snuck into a daydream.
            
            <fantasy>
              I’m leaning back with my feet propped on my desk when a pretty face with an agenda comes into my office. <think>Gee wiz,</think> I think to myself, <think>she sure is pretty.</think> “I have a problem, Miss Bachman.”

              I set my feet down to brace myself for the usual marital nonsense that is my bullshit and butter. “And I solve problems.” As she leans over my desk, her cleavage peaks out from her blouse; deep within my loins gurgles a wretched impulse, <feel>Fuck me fuck me fuck me!</feel>
              
              “Good. I need safe passage to Ecuador.”

              I cross my legs again to smother my other lips. “Not exactly my bread and bull—<emph>butter</emph>, but I can accomodate special requests. I’ll need a name, a real one—or assumed.”

              “I am Ann Animus.”

              “A dame with no name. A challenge.” Lou Peckinpaugh’s fatale curse, inverted.

              “I just told you that I go by ‘Ann Animus’. And I’m not a ‘dame’, I’m a femboy.”

              <feel>Cum inside me!</feel>

              <say>Oh, right. Sir.</say>

              <say>I prefer ‘ma’am’, actually.</say>

              <flashback>
                A broad is singing on the stage. She looks familiar. Androgynous face, boyish figure. She sings in a falsetto:
                
                <say>
                  <music time="4" beat="4" key="A" mode="minor" tempo="moderato" mood="resigned">
                    A4:1/4{You'll} | B4:1/4{have} C5{my} E5{heart} D5{and} | C5:1/4{eat} B4{it,} A4{too.}
                  </music>
                </say>

              </flashback>

              The songstress looks just like him. <think>Have I seen this transfixing transvestite before?</think> I try to remember what her voice sounded like while she wasn’t singing. <remember>Natural, but disciplined. She’s had voice training. It’s almost pure woman—but there’s a hint of manliness peaking through between her tits. “I’m Ann Animus, and I’m here all week!”</remember>

              That night at the club. I had been too drunk to remember my own name, let alone hers.

            </fantasy>

            “Andrea?”

            I snapped back into reality. “Wha?”

            It was Georgina. “Some ugly asshole wouldn’t stop pestering me last night. In unrelated news, I need you to run some plates for me and give me their address.”

            “Uh. Sure.”
          </narrative>

        </authors-note>

